there of course it is increasing talk  about how and when the lockdown could be  eased we'll be talking to the foreign  secretary Dominick Rob about the  government plans later but first we can  go to Devon where we can hear from a  medical expert the former deputy chief  medical officer Gina Radford who since  leaving that role last year has become a  vicar now of course we all know that  really the only long-term way out of the  lockdown  look at the lock down is a vaccine or  effective treatment how optimistic are  you that that can be found and how long  do you think it could take well I think  we need to be quite realistic about this  firstly we haven't at the moment got a  vaccine so we're having to start from  scratch we don't have a hugely good  track record with vaccines for this  particular virus coronavirus the family  of viruses but having said that  everything is being thrown at it there  are researchers all over the world  trying to identify back scenes so we've  never seen anything like the effort  that's being put to discover this  vaccine but even if we begin as we have  to identify things that might possibly  work vaccines that have potential and  there's also the huge process of testing  for there firstly whether they are  effective but also whether they're safe  because there's no point developing a  vaccine that actually is then going to  cause more harm than it's trying to  prevent though you know I think those  who are very used to the process of  developing vaccine are saying that  they're not anticipating is being  available until well into next year but  having said that with fast-tracking the  government's fast-tracking some testing  for example from Oxford so you never  know if that is successful maybe it'll  be with us sooner but then of course  we've got to manufacture it so you know  in the millions capacity certainly not a  quick process then as you say and now  I'm came to the auto pinion on a report  in the middle of Sunday today as well  saying that some British scientists have  developed these new immunity tests that  the ministers have ordered 50 million  off that could be ready in June these  are the tests that would effectively  show whether you've already had covered  19 and if you  to immunity do you think that sounds  realistic well we there are tests  already ailable and one of the issues is  not whether you know not that there  aren't tests available is actually how  accurate they are and so public health  England already been testing some and  what we call antibody tests and  unfortunately quality control measures  are not such that they are reliable so  whether this test is more reliable we  will have to see but the other issue we  need to put this whole question of  immunity in a little bit of perspective  we don't yet have evidence strong  evidence if you've had corona virus this  particular strain you are immune to  getting it again we don't know  you know what levels of the immunity of  antibody response are required and how  long the antibodies stay in the body so  we really don't know a lot about what it  means if you we do find antibodies in  people how long that's going to last so  that's why the government have set up  another research program to monitor a  large number of people over a long time  to see what happens to the antibodies in  their body it seems like a pretty  critical missing jigsaw piece that and  certainly listen to what you say it does  fellas have to lock down or some  measures of social distancing will be  here for a long time to come now of  course I know that what measures to ease  and when is a political decision but  would you be able to shadow make any  light on the kind of things that the  government is likely to be weighing on  well firstly whatever happens I  anticipate that the lockdown will be  lifted in two stages is not going to be  suddenly everyone's back to normal and I  think the message is getting through to  people that for many of us we're illness  for the longer term  but that they'll be looking at firstly  what's actually going on in the rest of  Europe from the rest of the world who  are ahead of us in terms of the epidemic  curve and we've seen for example that  what tends to happen is things like  small businesses being opened first if  they can safely socially distance  I'm social distancing is the key so  whether we would look at different types  of business maybe also different parts  of the country and also things like  schools we whether we can let children  go back but in a very safe way so maybe  reducing the numbers in a class maybe  having some classes going back one week  and other class going back another week  and they and their schooling being  supplemented by the continuing of online  classes so I think that the key message  is that whatever happens this is going  to be a staged process certainly  actually say be really important to look  at other countries and learn from the  lessons that we see there if you do look  around the world you can see that  certain countries seem to have done  frankly much better I guess and the UK  in being prepared for pandemic such as  the one we're seeing perhaps countries  like South Korea for example who have  experienced these respiratory pandemics  before but at the same time we've seen  what's happened with MERS and SARS even  if we didn't experience them so much  in the United Kingdom why was it that we  weren't better prepared for this why  didn't we stop Palmore PPE why don't we  invest in capacity for more testing why  don't we look down earlier what's the  answer  the answer is that all these things are  a balance aren't they because if you  stockpile masses and masses of PPE  firstly everything has an expiry date  let's be quite clear and also you need  the capacity to actually store this and  you then need the logic it's  underpinning that that means that you  can then deliver it and we know at the  beginning of this pandemic there were  some issues with actually even  delivering the stockpiles that we did  have the other issue is the difference  with this particular pandemic is not so  much that it is a pandemic it's awful as  that it it's actually the deadliness of  this virus so other and you know the  other sort of pandemics we tend to be  used to in this country like flu etc it  has not been as deadly as this one and  that is why it is caused such  an appropriately strong response from  what countries globally and put us into  lockdown so you know I thinking did  summarize we usually you know we do have  a strong track record of being prepared  for pandemics but this is a pandemic of  a completely different wave now just  finally while we've got you and you know  Vic a pretty big career change from  deputy chief medical officer during a  lockdown we've seen Christian celebrates  Easter Muslims Ramadan how difficult is  it for people of faith to acknowledge  these festivals in really difficult  times of social social distancing what  would your message be to them I think  the most important thing is to remember  that you know at the end of the day our  faith is central to who we are and we  celebrate and worship together in  whatever way we do but we can now  through the wonders and innovative and  creative ways that we are finding  worship and celebrate perhaps through  that the means of technology or the  phone or different ways that all of us  have a personal faith as well as a  communal faith and the most important in  all of this and all our faiths would say  this is that we have a responsibility  for each other we have a responsibility  to ensure that our own actions don't put  others at risk but certainly in my own  parish it's just been wonderful to see  the really creative and innovative ways  we've had a worshipping we have Xoom  services we have telephone services we  have zoom coffee mornings we come  together still as a community and also  the other important thing is the  pastoral side so people of all faiths  are coming together to show that support  to the wider community in very very  practical ways whether it be through  food banks that are helping with  distribution of food parcels and  medicines we all as people of faith have  a role to play in that

